# 2. Code Analysis & Search

Reconnaissance tools — use these to understand code before modifying it.

## TLDR-Swinton

Token-efficient code analysis with semantic search (~70-95% token savings). See https://github.com/mistakeknot/tldr-swinton

```bash
tldrs find "authentication logic"       # Semantic search (best for discovery)
tldrs index .                           # Build/update semantic index
tldrs context <function> --project .    # LLM-ready context for a function
tldrs structure src/                    # Show code structure (signatures)
tldrs calls .                           # Call graph (forward)
tldrs impact <function> .               # Find all callers (reverse)
tldrs slice <file> <func> <line>        # Isolate code affecting a line
```

| Task | Use |
|------|-----|
| Finding relevant code by concept | `tldrs find "description"` |
| Understanding unfamiliar code | `tldrs structure` |
| Pre-refactoring analysis | `tldrs calls` / `tldrs impact` |
| Actually modifying code | Read tool (need full source) |

**TLDR is for reconnaissance, Read is for surgery.**

Setup: `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mistakeknot/tldr-swinton/main/scripts/install.sh | bash`

## QMD (Local Search Engine)

On-device search for markdown knowledge bases. Indexed project roots typically include `/home/mk/projects` (server) and `/Users/sma/projects` (Mac). See https://github.com/tobi/qmd

```bash
qmd search "query"              # BM25 full-text search
qmd vsearch "query"             # Vector similarity (requires qmd embed)
qmd query "query"               # Combined search with reranking
qmd collection list             # List all collections
qmd update                      # Re-index all collections
qmd get <file>                  # Retrieve a document
```

Options: `-n <num>`, `--full`, `--json`, `-c <collection>`, `--files`

## Oracle CLI

Cross-AI review via GPT-5.2 Pro. Full reference: [`oracle-cli.md`](oracle-cli.md) (setup, options, examples, troubleshooting, patches).

**Essentials:** `oracle "<prompt>" --file <files...>` — both prompt and files required. Needs `DISPLAY=:99` and `CHROME_PATH=/usr/local/bin/google-chrome-wrapper`. If login expires: `oracle-login` via noVNC.

**Pre-flight:** Before every Oracle invocation, verify Xvfb is running (`pgrep -f "Xvfb :99"`). If Xvfb wasn't running when Oracle launched, the browser automation fails to connect to a display and silently times out. Restart Xvfb first (`Xvfb :99 -screen 0 1920x1080x24 &`), then retry.

## cass (Coding Agent Session Search)

Search previous Claude Code (and other agent) session histories. Useful for finding prior solutions, debugging context, or understanding what was done in past sessions. See https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/coding_agent_session_search

**Prefer Alwe** when it's available — Alwe (`~/.local/bin/alwe`, repo `os/Alwe`) wraps cass as MCP tools (`search_sessions`, `context_for_file`, `export_session`, `timeline`, `health`) and a friendlier CLI (`alwe search`, `alwe timeline --since 2h`). Use raw cass for index maintenance or when Alwe is missing.

```bash
cass search "query" --robot --limit 5   # Search with JSON output (agent-friendly)
cass search "query"                      # Search with human-readable output
cass view /path/to/session.jsonl -n 42   # View a specific message in a session
cass health --json                       # Check index status
cass index --full                        # Rebuild index (run if results seem stale)
cass context /path/to/file               # Find sessions related to a file
cass timeline --last 7d                  # Activity timeline for past week
```

**When to use:** Before investigating an unfamiliar area of the codebase, search for prior sessions that touched it. Past agents may have already solved the problem or documented important context.

